[SIZE=2][I]Part 3 of 4. Sections: 6. Real data, 7. Diagnostic tooling, 8. Design trade-offs, 9. Notes for modders[/I][/SIZE]

[SIZE=5][B]6. Real data[/B][/SIZE]

The 12.7% cited repeatedly above gets its provenance here: the full measurement methodology, the sample composition, and exactly where the conflict sits.

[B]6.1 The cone feasibility survey[/B]

Dataset: 11 debug saves from 1337.06 to 1743.04, spanning roughly 406 game years, [B]15,283 country-save pairs[/B] (saves from 1337-1338 carry 1,504-1,525 countries each, fewer in later consolidated states). Countries with no valid locations or all-zero targets were skipped.

Method: for each state, solve NNLS using the [B]finest model[/B] — one independent nonnegative coefficient per owned location. This matters: it is the theoretical ceiling for any classification scheme. The shipped solver has only 4 classes and far fewer degrees of freedom, so the rate measured here is an [B]optimistic bound[/B]; if even this fails, coarser classification necessarily fails harder.

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Metric[/TH][TH]Value[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]Scored country-save pairs[/TD][TD]15,283[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]Exact solve [B]feasible[/B][/TD][TD]1,940 ([B]12.7%[/B])[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]Exact solve [B]infeasible[/B][/TD][TD]13,343 ([B]87.3%[/B])[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]Median residual among infeasible[/TD][TD]0.129 - 0.243[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]Maximum residual among infeasible[/TD][TD]1.00 - 1.22[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

Median residuals sit above 0.13 — this is not "just barely missing", it is clearly out of reach.

Why: real economies carry a [B]structural conflict[/B] between income distribution and population structure. Noble-dense regions tend to also be where clergy concentrate (cities, seats of lordship), which makes the "nobles" and "clergy" columns highly correlated and flattens the cone. Once actual income breaks that correlation — wealthy nobles alongside strapped clergy — the target falls outside.

[B]Time trend[/B]: there isn't one. Feasibility stays in a narrow 12.5-13.2% band across 406 years (1337: 12.5%, 1338: 13.2%, later 1386/1536/1743 combined: 12.6%). Infeasibility is not a property of some historical phase, it is intrinsic to this modelling approach.

[B]6.2 Hungary across five saves: infeasibility is the steady state[/B]

Tracking Hungary over five consecutive saves (measured 2026-08-11, 189 owned locations). Again not using the class matrix but [B]one independent nonnegative factor per location[/B] — the theoretical limit of the reachable set, looser than any classification.

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Save[/TH][TH]All-location cone residual[/TH][TH]Most negative factor[/TH][TH]Locations needing x < 0[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]1337.10.02[/TD][TD]0.0504[/TD][TD]-6.03[/TD][TD]8[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]1337.11.02[/TD][TD]0.0558[/TD][TD]-5.95[/TD][TD]12[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]1337.12.01[/TD][TD]0.0465[/TD][TD]-5.75[/TD][TD]8[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]1338.01.13[/TD][TD][B]0.1057[/B][/TD][TD]-4.65[/TD][TD][B]43[/B][/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]1338.02.02[/TD][TD]0.0903[/TD][TD]-4.36[/TD][TD]37[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

[B]All five saves infeasible[/B], residuals oscillating between 0.0465 and 0.1057, and the count of locations needing negative factors climbing from 8 to 43. Not bad luck — a [B]persistent state[/B].

Decomposing by stratum subset pins the conflict precisely (residual 0.000 means that subset is reachable):

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Stratum subset[/TH][TH]Residual range[/TH][TH]Verdict[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]Single stratum (N / C / B / L individually)[/TD][TD]0.000[/TD][TD]All reachable[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]B + L (both need scaling down)[/TD][TD]0.000[/TD][TD]Same-direction pair reachable[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]N + C (both need scaling up)[/TD][TD]0.009 - 0.030[/TD][TD]Nearly reachable[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]N + L[/TD][TD]0.025 - 0.074[/TD][TD]Conflict[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]C + L[/TD][TD]0.026 - 0.106[/TD][TD]Conflict[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]C + B + L[/TD][TD]0.094 - 0.180[/TD][TD]Worst conflict[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]N + C + B + L (complete)[/TD][TD]0.049 - 0.109[/TD][TD]Infeasible[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

[B]The conflict structure is now clear[/B]: nobles and clergy need scaling up while lower needs scaling down substantially (its compression ratio falls to 0.586 by 1338). Satisfying either side alone is easy, same-direction combinations are easy, but [B]opposing combinations are impossible[/B] — those strata's per-location spending is coupled (nobles-vs-lower share correlation 0.29 to 0.47), so no nonnegative factor field separates them.

There's an extra lesson here. While diagnosing this, four plausible-sounding attributions were [B]all ruled out by data[/B]:

[LIST=1]
[*][B]"Zeros are being silently clamped"[/B] — a 0 returned by a nonnegative solve is a legitimate boundary optimum, not a clamping artefact
[*][B]"The matrix is ill-conditioned"[/B] — measured condition numbers were 55.6 to 96.0, perfectly healthy
[*][B]"The yearly unit-spending refresh triggers it"[/B] — saves 1337.10/11/12 had frozen unit spending and feasibility still moved
[*][B]"Classifier churn causes it"[/B] — running 1338 data under 1337's classes still gave Class 4 = -1.089
[/LIST]

The real cause is the structural conflict above, unrelated to algorithm, numerics, or classifier.

So the solver's design goal is not "raise the feasibility rate" (impossible) but [B]find the best compromise that is no worse than doing nothing, given confirmed infeasibility[/B] — which is precisely where section 5's hard constraint comes from.

[B]6.3 Large versus small countries: the original hunch, confirmed[/B]

Section 2's derivation already noted that severity depends on "the power distribution across locations and the relative counts of each location type" — more and more varied locations mean more room to manoeuvre. That was qualitative at the time. Measurement makes it concrete:

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Owned locations[/TH][TH]Countries[/TH][TH]Feasible[/TH][TH]Rate[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]1 - 9[/TD][TD]12,829[/TD][TD]1,292[/TD][TD][B]10.1%[/B][/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]10 - 49[/TD][TD]2,090[/TD][TD]466[/TD][TD]22.3%[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]50 - 199[/TD][TD]308[/TD][TD]147[/TD][TD]47.7%[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]200+[/TD][TD]56[/TD][TD]35[/TD][TD][B]62.5%[/B][/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

From 10.1% to 62.5% — [B]over a six-fold spread[/B]. Countries with more locations have more freedom to internally balance conflicting stratum needs; small countries simply don't have enough.

Worth noting the sample distribution itself: 12,829 of the 15,283 states (84%) are small countries with 1-9 locations. So that headline 87.3% infeasibility rate is substantially dragged down by a mass of minor states. For a 200+ location empire, the lived experience is that over six months in ten solve exactly.

Historically this makes sense — large empires did have more complex, more self-regulating internal markets. Something to watch on balance, though: large countries get both more land and a more accurate economic model.

[CENTER][IMG]FIGURE_5_URL[/IMG]
[SIZE=2][I]Figure 5: (a) overall feasible/infeasible split; (b) feasibility rate bucketed by location count; (c) Hungary's five-save all-location cone residual (red bars) and locations needing negative factors (blue line); (d) residual decomposition by stratum subset, where N/C/B/L are nobles/clergy/burghers/lower — single strata and same-direction pairs are reachable, opposing combinations conflict.[/I][/SIZE][/CENTER]

